NOTE ON REMOTION

The MIT licence above covers the effect source, prompts and tooling in this
repository. It does NOT cover Remotion itself, which is a dependency with its
own, different licence.

Remotion is free for individuals, non-profits, and for-profit organizations
with up to 3 employees. Larger for-profit organizations need a paid company
licence. See https://www.remotion.dev/license and node_modules/remotion/LICENSE.md

Copying an effect out of this repository is covered by MIT. Running it requires
Remotion, and that is between you and Remotion.

---

NOTE ON THE ASSETS IN public/

The MIT licence above covers the source, prompts and tooling. The media in public/
falls into two groups, and public/ASSETS.md is the authoritative record for both —
one row per file, with the source URL, the licence, and the command that derived it.

1. Generated assets — everything in public/audio/ and every public/*.svg — are
   original work produced by the scripts in scripts/. They are synthesised, not
   sampled, so there is no third-party licence and no attribution obligation.

2. public/footage/ is NASA video. NASA states that "NASA content - images, audio,
   video, and media files ... generally are not subject to copyright in the United
   States", and asks that "NASA should be acknowledged as the source of the
   material." NASA does not endorse this repository or anything built with it.

   Two caveats apply to the interview clips and are quoted in full in
   public/ASSETS.md: the NASA Insignia and identifiers are NOT in the public
   domain, and "if a NASA image, audio, video or media includes an identifiable
   person, using the media for commercial purposes may infringe that person's
   right of privacy or publicity, and permission should be obtained from the
   person." Both interview clips show an identifiable NASA astronaut wearing the
   insignia.

   These clips ship as demonstration assets. Read public/ASSETS.md and replace them
   with your own footage before using them in anything commercial, promotional or
   advertising-adjacent. Every effect that consumes footage takes its source path
   as a prop precisely so that swap is a one-line change.
